    "term": "CONTAMINANT",
    "fullName": "Contaminant",
    "description": "Any substance present on a runway surface that may adversely affect aircraft braking performance, directional control, or stopping distance. Under the ICAO Global Reporting Format (GRF), defined contaminants include: Dry Snow (density 50-200 kg/m³, freshly fallen low-moisture snow), Wet Snow (density 200-500 kg/m³, snow containing liquid water), Slush (density exceeding 500 kg/m³, snow/ice melted into fluid mixture presenting significant hydroplaning risk), Standing Water (liquid water depth >3mm), Ice (transparent/translucent layer bonded to pavement), Wet Ice (ice with liquid water film on surface, significantly more slippery), Frost (ice crystals formed by sublimation of water vapor onto surfaces below freezing), and Compacted Snow (snow compressed by rollers or aircraft traffic into dense hard layer). The contaminant assessment trigger is 10% coverage of any runway third. Contaminant depth thresholds are critical  3mm distinguishes acceptable braking from conditions requiring significant performance adjustments. Each contaminant type has known effects on aircraft braking behavior as determined through extensive flight testing by manufacturers.",
