    "term": "CDL",
    "fullName": "Configuration Deviation List",
    "description": "Configuration Deviation List (CDL) is a document approved by the aviation authority that identifies external airframe components (such as cowling panels, fairings, access doors, wingtip devices, and engine components) that may be missing or inoperative for a limited period while maintaining acceptable safety. Unlike the MEL which covers systems and equipment, the CDL addresses physical airframe configuration items. The CDL specifies: (a) the maximum allowable speed/Mach limitations with the missing component; (b) any performance penalties (increased fuel burn, reduced range); (c) any operational restrictions; and (d) the time limitation for repair. Missing CDL items typically require a logbook entry and may affect aircraft performance calculations including takeoff and landing distances. The CDL is published by the aircraft manufacturer and approved by the certifying authority, and is part of the Aircraft Flight Manual (AFM) or Operations Manual. Pilots must check the CDL during the pre-flight walkaround to ensure any missing items are properly deferred.",
