    "term": "LAHSO",
    "fullName": "Landing and Hold Short Operations",
    "description": "Landing and Hold Short Operations (LAHSO) is an air traffic control procedure that requires a pilot landing on one runway to hold short of an intersecting runway, taxiway, or other designated point. LAHSO is used at airports with intersecting runways to increase capacity by allowing simultaneous operations. The pilot-in-command has the final authority to accept or decline a LAHSO clearance. Before accepting, the PIC must determine that the aircraft can safely land and stop within the available landing distance (ALD), which is the distance from the runway threshold to the hold short point, and must consider: runway conditions (dry/wet/contaminated), braking action, wind conditions, aircraft weight, approach speed, reverse thrust capability, and elevation. LAHSO clearances are never issued when the visibility is less than 3 miles, or when the ceiling is below 1000 feet AGL at towered airports. A LAHSO clearance must be explicitly accepted by the pilot ('LAHSO accepted') and cannot be assumed. If a LAHSO clearance is not accepted, ATC must resequence traffic. LAHSO distances are published in airport diagrams and NOTAMs.",
