    "term": "SVFR",
    "fullName": "Special Visual Flight Rules",
    "description": "Special Visual Flight Rules (SVFR) are a set of aviation regulations that allow aircraft to operate within a control zone (Class B, C, D, or E airspace) in meteorological conditions less than the standard VFR minima but still within defined lower limits. SVFR requires: (a) ATC clearance; (b) the aircraft to remain clear of clouds; (c) flight visibility of at least 1 statute mile (some countries require 2 km / 1 NM); and (d) the pilot to be rated for instrument flight or hold a special VFR clearance. SVFR operations are typically limited to daylight hours unless the pilot holds an instrument rating and the aircraft is equipped for IFR flight. SVFR is intended for localized operations such as approaching/departing an airport when weather is below standard VFR but above SVFR minima. Fixed-wing SVFR is not authorized at night in some jurisdictions (e.g., FAA restricts night SVFR to helicopters only). SVFR clearances are issued on a 'first-come, first-served' basis and may be denied if traffic conditions preclude safe operations.",
