    "description": "Electronic Flight Bag (EFB) is an electronic device (typically a tablet, laptop, or dedicated device) used by flight crews to access and manage flight-related information, replacing traditional paper-based materials. EFBs are classified into three types by regulatory authorities: Type 1 (portable, commercially available devices used for document viewing), Type 2 (portable devices that can host EFB applications, may be connected to aircraft power), and Type 3 (installed equipment that is part of the aircraft configuration). EFB applications include: electronic charts and approach plates, flight manuals and checklists, performance calculations (takeoff/landing data, weight and balance), weather displays, NOTAM and flight planning, electronic logbooks, and aircraft systems monitoring. The FAA AC 120-76 and EASA AMC 20-25 provide regulatory guidance for EFB use. EFBs must be secured during taxi, takeoff, and landing, and must not interfere with aircraft systems. The use of EFBs has significantly reduced paper consumption in airline operations and improved access to real-time operational data.",
