    "term": "SCATANA",
    "fullName": "Security Control of Air Traffic and Air Navigation Aids",
    "description": "Security Control of Air Traffic and Air Navigation Aids (SCATANA) is a U.S. national security procedure that authorizes the military to assume control of civil air traffic and air navigation aids during national emergencies or security threats. SCATANA was originally established during World War II and has been invoked during major security events including the September 11, 2001 attacks. The procedures define three levels of airspace control: Alpha (selective restriction of air traffic), Bravo (extensive restrictions), and Charlie (complete airspace closure). Under SCATANA, the Department of Defense (through NORAD/USNORTHCOM) coordinates with the FAA to implement airspace restrictions, including: ground stops, diversion of airborne aircraft, closure of airspace sectors, and deactivation of navigation aids. The FAA Order 7610.4 (Special Military Operations) details SCATANA implementation procedures. SCATANA authority is distinct from TFRs (Temporary Flight Restrictions) which are typically issued for localized events.",
