    "description": "Category IIIB (CAT IIIB) is an instrument approach and landing operation using precision approach procedures (typically ILS or GLS) with a Decision Height (DH) below 50 feet (15m) or no DH, and a Runway Visual Range (RVR) of less than 700 feet (200m) but not less than 300 feet (75m). CAT IIIB requires fail-operational autoland with automatic rollout guidance to taxi speed, redundant flight control computers and autopilot systems, airport certification with centerline lights, touchdown zone lights, high-intensity approach lighting, RVR sensors at all three runway positions, and comprehensive crew training in automatic landing, rollout, and missed approach procedures. At CAT IIIB minima, the aircraft can complete the landing and decelerate to taxi speed with the autopilot engaged. CAT IIIB operations are the highest category commonly approved for commercial aviation (CAT IIIC with zero RVR is rarely implemented).",
