    "term": "MOR",
    "fullName": "Mandatory Occurrence Report",
    "description": "Mandatory Occurrence Report (MOR) is a formal report submitted by aviation personnel (pilots, engineers, cabin crew, ground handlers) to the national aviation authority detailing specific safety-related events that must be reported by law or regulation. Under ICAO Annex 13, EASA Part 21 and Part 145, and national regulations, mandatory occurrences include: (a) aircraft accidents and serious incidents; (b) technical defects affecting airworthiness; (c) failures of critical systems (engines, flight controls, landing gear); (d) fires, explosions, or smoke events; (e) bird strikes causing significant damage; (f) ground collisions; (g) operational incidents (runway incursions, airspace infringements); and (h) cargo-related incidents (dangerous goods leaks, shifts). MORs are submitted to the national authority (e.g., FAA, EASA, CAA, KOTI) within 72 hours of the event. MOR data feeds into broader safety databases such as ICAO ADREP, ECCAIRS, and the IATA GADM for global trend analysis.",
