    "term": "TOGA",
    "fullName": "Takeoff/Go Around",
    "description": "TOGA (Takeoff/Go Around) refers to both the takeoff thrust setting and the go-around procedure. As a thrust mode, TOGA provides maximum takeoff thrust for takeoff (reduced thrust may be used below TOGA) and maximum go-around thrust for missed approach or balked landing. As a procedure, the TOGA maneuver is initiated by pressing the TOGA buttons (typically located on the thrust levers) and involves: (a) applying TOGA thrust; (b) rotating to go-around attitude (typically 10-15 degrees nose up); (c) configuring the aircraft (flaps retract from landing to go-around setting); (d) positive rate of climb  gear up; (e) following the missed approach procedure; and (f) coordinating with ATC. The decision to go around is one of the most critical safety decisions in aviation; late or hesitant go-around decisions are a contributing factor in many approach and landing accidents. Modern SOPs emphasize a low threshold for go-around decisions with a stabilized approach criteria (stable by 1,000 feet IMC, 500 feet VMC).",
