    "term": "EMAS",
    "fullName": "Engineered Materials Arresting System",
    "description": "Engineered Materials Arresting System (EMAS) is a passive safety system installed at the end of runways to safely decelerate aircraft that overrun the runway end. EMAS consists of blocks of lightweight, crushable cellular concrete (or similar engineered materials) that are designed to collapse under the weight of an aircraft, creating drag that slows and stops the aircraft. EMAS is typically 300-600 feet long and is designed to stop an aircraft up to approximately 80 knots exit speed. EMAS is installed at airports where the standard Runway Safety Area (RSA) of 1,000 feet cannot be provided due to terrain, water, roads, or other constraints. The FAA requires EMAS at commercial service airports where the RSA is deficient. EMAS has successfully stopped over 20 aircraft since its introduction, preventing potentially catastrophic overrun accidents. EMAS beds must be maintained (repaired after use) and are typically replaced after a single aircraft engagement. EMAS is distinct from RESA (Runway End Safety Area), which is a cleared, graded area.",
