    "term": "ARFF",
    "fullName": "Aircraft Rescue and Fire Fighting",
    "description": "Aircraft Rescue and Fire Fighting (ARFF) is the emergency response service provided at airports to respond to aircraft accidents, incidents, and fires. ARFF services are governed by ICAO Annex 14 (Aerodromes), FAA 14 CFR Part 139 (Certification of Airports), and EASA ADR.OPS. ARFF requirements are based on the airport's aircraft category (determined by the longest aircraft using the airport), defining: (a) minimum number of ARFF vehicles; (b) agent quantities (water, foam, dry chemical, Halon alternatives); (c) response time (typically 3 minutes to any point on the runway, 2-3 minutes for CAT 7-10 airports); (d) staffing levels; and (e) training requirements. ARFF personnel are trained in aircraft fire behavior, rescue techniques, hazardous materials response, aircraft systems familiarization, and emergency evacuation procedures. ARFF vehicles carry specialized equipment including turret nozzles (roof-mounted, bumper-mounted), piercing nozzles for fuselage penetration, thermal imaging cameras, hydraulic rescue tools, and breathing apparatus.",
